course: chemistry
lesson: 9
title: Changes you can undo and changes you can't
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some changes can be undone: melt chocolate and let it set, and it is chocolate again; fold a paper aeroplane and unfold it, and it is still paper.
explain: Others cannot: fry an egg, toast bread or burn paper, and you will never get the runny egg, the soft bread or the white page back.
explain: The difference is whether new substances are made: melting and folding only move particles about, but cooking and burning make atoms join up in new ways.
explain: Chemists look for clues that something new has formed: a new colour, bubbles of gas, light or heat, a new smell, or a change that will not go back.
explain: The clues can trick you, though: boiling water bubbles, yet it is still water.
fact: Melting, freezing, boiling and condensing are reversible changes, because the molecules stay the same.
fact: Chocolate that melts and sets again may get a pale grey coat because fat comes to the surface; it is still safe to eat.
fact: Burning paper makes new substances (carbon dioxide, water vapour and ash) by joining the paper's atoms with oxygen; the atoms are not destroyed.
fact: Heat unfolds the long protein molecules in egg white so they tangle together for good; scientists were reported to partly "unboil" egg proteins with a special machine, but not in a kitchen.
fact: Baking a cake sets proteins, makes gas bubbles that make it rise, and browns sugars; a cake taken out too early can sink.
fact: Folding, bending and cutting change only shape; they are physical changes, not chemical ones.
fact: Toast turns brown because sugars and proteins react into new substances; burnt toast is black because it has partly turned into carbon.
fact: Baking clay in a kiln changes its minerals for good, so pottery does not turn back into mud and can last thousands of years.
fact: A firework is a very fast chemical reaction that makes hot gases, light, heat, noise and smoke.
fact: Fireworks cause serious injuries every year; they are only for trained grown-ups, and nobody should go back to one that did not go off.
fact: Clues to a chemical change are a new colour, gas bubbles, light or heat, a new smell, or something that will not change back.
fact: A clue alone can mislead: boiling water bubbles but no new substance forms, and a colour change can also be just paint or mixing.
exercise: You melt chocolate and let it set. What kind of change is that? | A change you can undo | A change you can never undo | A change that makes new substances | A change that destroys the chocolate
exercise: Why can't you get a burnt piece of paper back? | Burning made new substances, gas and ash | The flame destroyed the paper's atoms | The paper is hiding inside the smoke | The fire ate the paper's atoms
exercise: Why can't a fried egg turn back into a runny egg? | Heat unfolded the proteins and they tangled for good | The egg white turned to plastic | The egg lost all its water | The yolk sucked up the white
exercise: Why can't you get the eggs and flour back from a baked cake? | Heat made new substances and set the proteins | The cake is only a mixture, so they are all still there to pick out | The ingredients floated away | The oven ate them
exercise: You fold a paper aeroplane and unfold it. What kind of change is that? | A change of shape you can undo | A chemical change that makes a new substance | A change that makes new paper | A change you can never undo
exercise: Why does toast turn brown? | Sugars and proteins react into new brown substances | The bread gets dirty in the toaster | Brown paint melts out of the bread | The bread dries out and nothing else
exercise: Why does a fired clay pot not turn back into mud in water? | The kiln's heat changed and fused its minerals | It is covered all over with a thin coat of plastic | Water cannot touch pots | Clay is waterproof before firing
exercise: Ice melts, then the water freezes again. What does that show? | Changes of state can be undone | Water is destroyed when it melts | Ice and water are different substances | Freezing makes brand new molecules
exercise: What happens to the chemicals inside a firework? | They react and are used up | They are saved up for the next fireworks display | They turn back into a grey powder | They melt, then set hard again
exercise: Which is a clue that a new substance has formed? | Gas bubbles, light or a new smell appearing | Something being bent or folded into a new shape | Ice turning into water | A liquid being poured into a cup
exercise: Why does old chocolate sometimes look whitish? | Fat has come to the surface after melting and setting | It has gone mouldy and poisonous | It has turned into sugar | It was painted at the factory
exercise: Is cutting paper a physical change or a chemical change? | Physical: still paper | A chemical change, it is a new substance | Neither, nothing happens | Both kinds of change at once
exercise: Why should you never go back to a firework that did not go off? | It could still go off suddenly and injure you | It is out of fuel and harmless | It turns into a smoke bomb | It belongs to someone else, and only the owner may pick it up
exercise: Burnt toast is black. What is the black stuff? | Carbon left after the bread partly burns | Ash from the hot toaster wires | Paint from the bread bag | Pepper sprinkled on it at the bakery
test: Your friend says: "Bubbles mean a new substance, so boiling water is a chemical change." Put him right. | The bubbles are water vapour, still water, so it is reversible | He is right, boiling breaks the old water up and makes brand new water from it | Boiling makes hydrogen and oxygen | The bubbles are air from the kettle
test: "if atoms never get destroyed, why can't I glue my burnt homework back together?" Best answer? | The atoms are now in new substances in the air and ash | The atoms were all destroyed by the fire, so there is nothing left to glue | The paper is hiding in the smoke | You can, with strong enough glue
test: What would happen if you opened the oven far too early while a cake was rising? | It could sink, as bubbles collapse before the walls are firm | It would rise twice as fast, because the cool air from the kitchen helps it grow | It would turn back into batter at once | Nothing at all would change
test: A cartoon chef "un-fries" an egg by putting it in the fridge. What is wrong? | Cooling cannot undo a cooked egg | Nothing, eggs uncook when they get cold | It would only work in a very cold freezer | The cold egg would turn into a chick
test: Your sister says: "A colour change always means a chemical change." Is that right? | No, mixing paints changes colour without new substances | Yes, every colour change is chemical, even mixing paints | Yes, but only for red | No, colour never changes in chemistry
test: Why do archaeologists find so many broken pots? | Fired pottery does not rot or dissolve, so it lasts thousands of years | Ancient people broke all of their old pots on purpose every year, for good luck | Pots grow in the ground | Clay always turns into pots in soil
test: "can't I just scrape off the burnt bits and get my bread back?" Best answer? | The toasted layer is new substances, the bread can't come back | Yes, scraping off the brown layer turns it straight back into soft bread | Yes, if you add water | Only for white bread
test: Grandma's chocolate bar looks pale and grey. Your brother says it's poison. What is the truth? | It is fat bloom from melting and setting, still safe | He is right, the whole cupboard of food must be thrown away | It is mould that glows | It is chalk added by the shop
test: Which of these is a change you can undo? | Water vapour cooling back into liquid water | Paper burning to ash | Bread being toasted | A firework exploding in the sky
test: Is boiling water a reversible change? | Yes, steam condenses back to water | No, boiling destroys the water for good, so it is gone | No, steam is a totally new substance | Only high up on a tall mountain top
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a reversible change? | A change in which no new substance is formed and the original may be recovered | A change in which new substances are formed by the rearrangement of atoms within molecules | A change accompanied invariably by the emission of light | A change in which atoms are destroyed
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the frying of an egg cannot be reversed in the kitchen. | The proteins have unfolded and become permanently entangled | The egg has lost its entire mass to the surrounding air | The yolk has been converted to metal | The egg has been frozen solid
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the combustion of paper. | New substances are produced, the atoms of the paper being combined with oxygen | The atoms of the paper are wholly annihilated by the heat of the flame and cease to exist | The paper is converted into pure water | The paper may be restored by cooling the ash
exam: It is given that bubbles are observed in a liquid. Determine whether this observation alone establishes that a chemical change has occurred. | It does not, since boiling water bubbles without forming a new substance | It does, in all cases without exception, bubbles being proof of a new substance | It does, provided the liquid is water | It does, provided the vessel is metal
